Z||000000||Greg Piet||12-09-1999||11:50 AM||||OK at this site you can use the net to place free US long distance(You can actually call a real telephone).  This will save you money on phone bills which will let you spend more money on fixing up your Fiero!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.dialpad.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dialpad.com</A> <P>I used it last night at 33kbps and it worked pretty good!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||olympic||12-09-1999||01:04 PM||||Here in Saskatchewan we have the only government owned telephone company left in North America. They ripped us off for alot of years but since Sprint and AT&T are competing things are pretty good. For $80 a month I get my line rental, unlimited local calling, unlimited Canadian long distance, unlimited internet access and a bunch of calling features(call waiting, etc.) I've seen lots of U.S. ads for per minute plans but do any companys offer flat rate unlimited plans like we have??||||reg||14
Z||000002||Patrick||12-09-1999||01:33 PM||||<BR>Unless you do one heck of a lot of long distance calls, or stay on-line all day, $80 a month sounds pretty expensive to me. In BC, my phone bill (including call display) and my separate ISP (120 hours) bill add up to half that. And we're <B>both</B> talking Canadian dollars here.||||reg||4
Z||000003||olympic||12-09-1999||02:00 PM||||Patrick,<BR>   Your line rental in BC must be cheaper than here. SaskTel still has the monopoly on local service and they charge to much for it. The unlimited long distance I get adds about $40 to my bill but it is worth it to me. My  family and I use over 800 minutes of long distance every month. Even at $0.10 per minute it would cost me twice as much.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||12-09-1999||06:08 PM||||ive gotten my phone bill up to 200 dollars from long distance.  ATT plan.  Its a quarter during the day and at night its a eleven cents.  Its cheaper using a calling card for me.  My internets free because its a local call and the server is less then 7 miles away from me.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||03:33 AM||||What's the catch?  Anything?  Do you have to change your long distance carrier or something?||||reg||5
Z||000006||Greg Piet||12-10-1999||04:16 AM||||The only "catch" I know of is that in the virtual phone window, banners get cycled through a little window in the phone window.  They say they pay the phone bill through their advertisers, so I am guessing that clicking on the banners will help them stay in business.  <P>I dont work for them, but the site has a detailed explanation as to how it works.  I was also thinking that since it was on the net, anyone in the world should be able to use it to make calls in the united states. (I dont know if this would break their use agreement though)<P>I used it during peak hours and it got a bit choppy, but then again my daytime connection has a tendency to stall due to excessive use .||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||12-10-1999||11:16 AM||||FieroSpeeder, <P>ATT is giving you the big shaft!  I called ATT about a billing error and asked what the new rates were.  I got .07 from 7am-7pm and .05 from 7pm-7am with a monthly charge of 4.95.  Thats the same monthly charge as all of the other plans so its a deal and a half.  Call and have yourself switched.<P>AS far as internet calling goes, I use to have AccessPower (just add the www and the .com for thier website) and it worked well for a while, but the service quality went down as their subscribership went up.  It was  $10 a month for unlimited  calling in N. America.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-10-1999||01:07 PM||||I'll have to check it out.  If I can make free international calls, I'll have to use it to call Cliff and thank him for stuff.  Thanx for letting us know about it.||||reg||1
